:S4_133 a dm:StoryCase ;
    dm:storyId "S4-133" ;
    dm:storySection "4.13.2" ;
    dm:storyKind "private letter ruling" ;
    dm:pageSays "A non-qualified option given to charity gives the employee ordinary income at exercise with no offsetting deduction, the page concludes." ;
    dm:citesAuthority "PLRs 9616035, 9349004, 9737014–16" ;
    dm:pageOutcome dm:Block ;
    dm:storyScope dm:OutsideModel ;
    dm:outsideCategory dm:OutrightGiftToPublicCharity ;
    dm:outsideReason "gift of stock options" ;
    dm:pageAccuracyNote "The notes say the page's own linked rulings (S4-135, S4-136) contradict this conclusion." ;
    dm:citedForOpposite "PLRs 200141018 and 200202034, read 2026-11-21 on irs.gov: both are about a corporation pledging an option on its own stock to a private foundation. A pledge alone gives no deduction; the deduction comes when the charity exercises, roughly the share value less the exercise price. Neither is about an employee's options, so the page is wrong to say no deduction ever arises, and the rulings do not settle the employee case. Private letter rulings are not precedent (6110(k)(3), read)." ;
    dm:citedAuthorityRead true .

